Coastal wetlands, including salt marshes, mangroves, and swamps, act as natural buffers against the destructive forces of storms. While they do not make coastal communities invincible, they significantly mitigate flood damage through several distinct physical mechanisms.

The primary mechanism is energy dissipation. When storm surges and high waves move from the open ocean toward the shore, they must pass through the dense vegetation of wetlands. The stems, leaves, and roots of plants like cordgrass or mangrove trees create friction, slowing down the water. This reduction in velocity decreases the wave height and the force with which water hits the shoreline